      "problem": "Express $\\ln\\left(x^2 y\\right)$ as a sum of two logarithms.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
